WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:09.020
"The worse case scenario is that Britain ends
up as a kind of Singapore on the Edge of Europe,

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:11.450
competing on the basis of deregulation."

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:20.380
"I'm Frances O’Grady.

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:26.909
I'm the General Secretary of the TUC, which is the umbrella organization for trade unions in the UK."

00:00:26.909 --> 00:00:28.199
 

00:00:28.199 --> 00:00:34.730
"For me Brexit represents a real risk to workers' jobs, their livelihoods and their rights at work."

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:35.730
 

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:38.900
"Let's be brave, let's think big and let's
organize."

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:44.330
"The prime minister is very keen on red lines.

00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:46.130
She's got lots of them.

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:51.510
She's ruled out staying in the customs union and in the single market, which would protect jobs."

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:52.510
 

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:58.370
"My argument is that working people across
Europe have an interest in defending a level

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:00.370
playing field on workers rights.

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:07.330
What we don’t want to see is firms using
British workers to undercut German or French workers

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:09.970
and end up in a race to the bottom."

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:25.800
"I met Mr. Barnier today and his message was
very clear that if the prime minister of Britain

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:32.340
sticks to her red lines, of coming out of
the single market and customs union,

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:36.450
then all we're left with is the option of a free trade deal,

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:42.670
and that poses real threats to workers' rights,
their livelihoods and their jobs."

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:50.430
"Some of the hard Brexiteers including in
the Conservative Party see Brexit as an opportunity

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:56.280
to slash and burn rights and standards that
trade unionists have fought for over generations.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:59.979
And our job is to stop them.

00:01:59.979 --> 00:02:05.479
These rights that we combine together as unions
across Europe to win, like maternity rights,

00:02:05.479 --> 00:02:10.250
equal pay for work of equal value, these are
rights that really matter."

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:19.210
"I think we have to make compromises, and
that's also because when I look at the alternatives,

00:02:19.210 --> 00:02:21.460
whose rules would I rather play by?

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:27.349
I would rather play by the rules of the single
market and the customs union – imperfect

00:02:27.349 --> 00:02:34.180
though it may be – than be told by President
Trump what should happen to our steel industry."

